            <ArticleTitle>Gradenigo syndrome: a case-report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>43-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case of sixth nerve palsy as a rare complication of acute otitis media (apical petrositis). The clinical triad of acute otitis media, pain in the distribution of the fifth cranial nerve and sixth nerve palsy is known as Gradenigo syndrome.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
